{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Investigation + production repair (2026-11-11, PST).\n\nObservation (prod clone /var/gerrit/site/mcp-tickets, git 2.47.3 in the MCP container):\n- Before: 78,737 loose objects (~9.7 GB loose on disk), 235 packs, 2.27 GB packed; commit-graph last written 2026-08-31.\n- `.git/objects/maintenance.lock` and `.git/gc.pid` both dated 2026-09-03 01:36:18 UTC (2026-09-02 18:36 PDT) — stranded since then. 12 `fetch-pack` `.keep` files from 2026-09-05..09-15 (killed fetches).\n- Reproduced in the prod container on git 2.47.3 (scratch repo): with a maintenance.lock present, `git maintenance run --auto` exits 0 and does nothing; removing it makes the same command pack. Upstream git never expires maintenance.lock (confirmed in upstream patch thread + several downstream reports).\n- rebar's deferred step `run_auto_maintenance` runs exactly `git maintenance run --auto`, so every write since 2026-09-03 got a silent no-op.\n- Live, within ~30 s of removing the stale lock: rebar-mcp (container PID 1) adopted an ORPHANED `git maintenance run --auto --quiet --no-detach` -> `gc --auto` -> `repack --cruft` + `prune`, running outside rebar's write lock (I was holding it). Its parent was a git porcelain process that had already died. Locally confirmed: `git fetch` and `git merge` each spawn `git maintenance run --auto` as a child; rebar suppresses that only on commits (`_AUTOMAINT_OFF`), not on sync/push fetch (30 s bound) or merge (120 s bound).\n- A full repack of this store took 605.6 s and 2.58 GB RSS. It cannot finish under either bound.\n\nRepair (operator-authorized prod write), under rebar's own `write_lock` in a one-off container from the same image (no cgroup cap):\n- Backup: `cp -a .git` -> `/var/lib/rebar/backups/mcp-tickets-gitdir-20261111T192528Z` (12 GB; `git rev-parse --git-dir` resolves to the copy; all 3 refs identical to live). It is a standalone gitdir, not a worktree pointer.\n- Removed the stranded maintenance.lock, gc.pid and the 12 stale `.keep` files; `git repack -a -d -l` (605.6 s). I deliberately aborted my own `prune --expire=now` (fetches run outside the write lock, so expire=now could race a fetch's fresh loose objects); the orphaned gc then ran git's own `prune --expire 2.weeks.ago` and rewrote commit-graph.\n- After: 10 loose objects, 2 packs (1 + cruft), 1.1 GB objects dir. Refs unchanged.\n- History walk timings (3 runs each, same commands): `git log -- <ticket-dir>` 104.9/116.6/113.4 s -> 28.4/23.2/23.4 s; `git log --name-only -n 5000` 32.5/25.5/21.0 s -> 4.1/3.0/3.1 s; `rev-list --count HEAD` ~0.9 s -> ~0.55 s. fsck connectivity check pending.\n\nNext: durable code fix in progress (worktree fix-gonolek)."},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1794426200826552855,"uuid":"7b60991c-52ec-46bb-a483-15516da9cca6"}